    "explanation": "To both parties: this was an upfront-quoted trip, so the price for distance and time was fixed at booking. The fare breakdown (EV-FARE-00524, weight 1.0) records a quoted fare of SGD 12.90 and a total actually charged of SGD 16.10, a difference of SGD 3.20, with payment_status CHARGED and refund_issued_sgd 0.00. No pass-through or statutory line absorbs that difference - tolls/ERP are SGD 0.00, the airport surcharge is SGD 0.00 and the waiting-time fee is SGD 0.00 - so under POL-RD-11(a) the SGD 3.20 is refunded in full. That figure, charged_total less quoted_fare, is the one I used. The driver is not penalised for route deviation: the GPS trace (EV-GPS-00524) records 3.37 km driven against an optimal 3.30 km, an excess of 0.07 km or 2.12%, and neither POL-RD-01 gate (more than 0.8 km, more than 20%) is met, so no deviation is established and POL-RD-02 does not run on an upfront trip. The driver's suggestion of 'traffic at the last junction' is backed by no traffic advisory - EV-ADVIS-00524-01 is MISSING, weight 0.0 - and exonerates nothing; with no deviation found there is nothing to exonerate. The driver's request for NO_ACTION is rejected: a deviation finding is not a precondition of the POL-RD-11(a) remedy, and the amount charged above the quote must come back whether or not the route deviated. The rider's belief that the fare reverted to SGD 12.90 the moment the dispute was filed is not borne out - the ledger still shows CHARGED and no refund issued - and the rate-card reconciliation the rider offers is not needed, because the refund follows from the quote-versus-charge difference itself. No repeat-deviation pattern exists (deviation_flags_90d 0 over 250 completed_trips_90d), so no POL-RD-06 uplift or conduct referral arises; no unmatched gantry or misapplied surcharge exists, so POL-RD-09(4) produces no billing-integrity referral."
